## Corrected Solution

```java
public class PairCounter {

    public static int countPairs(int[] arr) {
        return countPairsRecursive(arr, 0);
    }

    private static int countPairsRecursive(int[] arr, int index) {
        // Base case: stop when there's no element after the current one
        if (index >= arr.length - 1) {  // ✅ FIX: use length - 1
            return 0;
        }

        // Check if current element is twice the next element
        int count = (arr[index] == 2 * arr[index + 1]) ? 1 : 0;

        // Recurse on the rest
        return count + countPairsRecursive(arr, index + 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] test = {4, 2, 8, 4, 6, 3};
        System.out.println(countPairs(test)); // Prints 2
    }
}
```

### Why `arr.length - 1` Works

| Array length | Last index | When `index >= length - 1` triggers | Safe? |
|--------------|------------|-------------------------------------|-------|
| 0 | N/A | Immediately at index 0 | ✅ |
| 1 | 0 | At index 0 (no pair possible) | ✅ |
| 6 | 5 | At index 5 (no element after last) | ✅ |

