## Key Takeaway

When a recursive function accesses `arr[index + k]`, the base case must ensure `index + k` is still in bounds. Here, since we access `arr[index + 1]`, we need `index + 1 < arr.length`, i.e., `index < arr.length - 1`, i.e., `index >= arr.length - 1` triggers the base case. Always double-check boundary conditions — they are the most common source of off-by-one errors in recursion!

